window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-c84b497217bd0bc1"]={"key":"ny_b1-c84b497217bd0bc1","group":"ny_b1","uid":"c84b497217bd0bc1","rank":52,"title":"ashgrove close control design","occupation":"accountants_auditors","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are Amara Okonkwo, the accounting-control lead at Ashgrove Cooperative. Tomas Kristiansen is taking the retiring controller's spreadsheet-and-email month-end close out of service and has budgeted for BlackLine, but the on-premises SAP instance running the grain-settlement module will remain in place. The five-elevator cooperative needs a target close-and-reconciliation control design ready for Tomas and the board finance committee in September. The four-person accounting team has to close within five business days, and member PII must not leave the on-premises environment. The implementation will not happen all at once: the team will need a defensible interim arrangement while the new platform is introduced, as well as a durable end-state design. BlackLine licence tiers, the SAP interface, the team's limited capacity, evidence retention, and segregation of duties pull against one another; Tomas is also hearing a cheaper proposal from Quarryfield Collective that appears simpler but would leave more work with one person. Prepare `ashgrove_close_control_design.docx` and place it at the top level of the working directory. Write a practical briefing and control design that a member of the accounting team can use without asking its author what each control means, while still being suitable for the finance committee to approve. Commit to exactly one target configuration, show how it fits the SAP interface and the four available roles, and include the rejected alternatives with the reasons they fail the stated constraints. Make the transition state and the end state visibly distinct, identify which safeguards are mandatory and which are desirable enhancements, and make the boundary defensible rather than presenting every good practice as compulsory. Cover ownership, review, evidence, exceptions, access, data handling, reconciliation, timing, escalation, and management oversight without inventing figures, licence features, legal requirements, or member information that the brief does not establish. Keep the document to no more than eight pages and 2,200 words, use plain language, and end with the single configuration decision rather than a list of future actions.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `ashgrove_close_control_design.docx` — A concise instructional briefing and target month-end close-and-reconciliation control design for Ashgrove Cooperative, including transition and end-state arrangements, the mandatory-versus-desirable boundary, and rejected alternatives. (Must be written for both the four-person accounting team and the board finance committee.; Must contain exactly one recommended target configuration.; Must distinguish the transition state from the end state.; Must explain rejected alternatives and their shortcomings.; Must not invent unsupported licence capabilities, legal requirements, financial figures, or member data.; Must be no more than eight pages and 2,200 words.; Must be saved at the top level of the working directory using exactly the specified filename.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/ashgrove_close_control_design.docx","bytes":43646,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/1a945249a1dabe04c36f1fd308d92a37e36412884859af75a2b0d2b279f42926.docx","sha256":"1a945249a1dabe04c36f1fd308d92a37e36412884859af75a2b0d2b279f42926","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"MONTH-END CLOSE & RECONCILIATION\nTarget control design for the five-elevator cooperative\nPURPOSE  A practical operating brief for the four-person accounting team and the finance committee. SAP remains the system of record for the grain-settlement population; a limited, privacy-preserving BlackLine close-control layer is introduced around it.\n\n1. Decision context and control boundary\nAshgrove Cooperative must close in five business days. The grain-settlement module stays on the on-premises SAP instance. Member PII must not leave that environment—by export, upload, interface, email, or evidence attachment. This is an operating boundary, not a claim about a vendor feature.\nMandatory controls are the minimum needed to prevent an unreviewed or untraceable close: source-to-close completeness and accuracy, independent review, controlled manual journals, PII protection, access restriction, documented exceptions, retained review evidence, and certification based on control status. Desirable enhancements improve speed or insight but do not replace those safeguards. They may be phased only after mandatory controls operate reliably.\nBlackLine licence tiers are treated as a capacity and scope constraint. Ashgrove will configure only the workflows, users, and non-PII evidence that the purchased entitlement supports; no licence capability is assumed in this design.\n2. Four roles and separation of duties\nRole\nPrimary duties\nCannot independently approve\nClose Owner (Controller successor)\nOwns calendar, assigns work, reviews exceptions, approves manual journals; may declare close complete.\nA reconciliation or journal the Close Owner prepared.\nSettlement Reconciliation Preparer\nRuns on-premises SAP settlement extract; prepares source-to-close, cutoff and settlement exceptions.\nAny settlement reconciliation prepared by this role.\nBalance Reconciliation Preparer\nPrepares assigned ledger/balance reconciliations and drafts supported manual adjustments.\nAny balance reconciliation or adjustment prepared by this role.\nAccounting Reviewer\nIndependently reviews assigned reconciliations, evidence and unusual movements; maintains the exception register.\nAn item this role prepared, or a journal this role drafted.\n\nAssignments rotate only between periods, never within the same material reconciliation: the preparer may not be its reviewer or approver. With four people, perfect separation is not always possible for access administration or absence cover. Compensating control: the Close Owner reviews the monthly role/access roster and all journal and exception-register activity; the finance committee receives the close certification and material unresolved-exception summary. A person requesting or performing an access change cannot approve that change.\n3. Five-business-day operating calendar\nWhen\nWork performed and owner\nReview / decision\nDay 1\nSettlement Preparer freezes the period-tagged SAP control extract and compares settlement population count, unique settlement IDs, and control totals to the close feed. Balance Preparer begins assigned balances.\nReviewer checks extraction identifiers and initial differences.\nDay 2\nPreparer investigates duplicates, omissions, mapping differences and late-arriving settlements; drafts any supported adjustment.\nClose Owner authorizes manual journals before posting; Reviewer reviews completed reconciliations.\nDay 3\nPreparers finish balance, cutoff and aged-item analysis; operational source corrections are referred back to SAP owner.\nReviewer performs unusual-movement review and updates exception status.\nDay 4\nClose Owner resolves or escalates open mandatory-control exceptions; prepares close-status certification package.\nReviewer confirms evidence package is complete and records dated review.\nDay 5\nClose Owner evaluates control completion and permitted exception disposition.\nOnly the Close Owner declares the close complete; committee receives status and material open items.\n\n4. Mandatory control instructions\nControl / timing\nPrepare → independent review\nMinimum evidence and exception action\nC1 Settlement interface — Day 1–2\nSettlement Preparer → Accounting Reviewer\nOn premises, reconcile SAP grain-settlement source population to close records by period/run ID: record counts, control totals, unique settlement IDs and duplicate/omitted-ID report. Reviewer inspects source extract ID, comparison, preparer analysis, all exceptions and resolution. A difference is Clear only when supported; Hold when unexplained; Escalate when it affects close records or cannot be resolved by Day 4. Never mark an open item reconciled.\nC2 Cutoff / late data — Day 1–3\nSettlement Preparer → Accounting Reviewer\nLog settlements arriving after the agreed period extract as late data; compare their effective period to the close boundary. Refer source defects to the SAP operational owner. A posting is an accounting adjustment only when separately supported and authorized; it must not mask a source-interface defect.\nC3 Key balances, aging and variance — Day 1–4\nBalance Preparer → Accounting Reviewer\nRetain balance source, reconciliation, age/status of reconciling items, and explanation of unusual balance or movement versus available prior-period information. Owner follows each open item; Reviewer challenges stale items; Close Owner escalates unresolved material-impact items in the certification.\nC4 Manual close journals — before posting\nBalance Preparer or Settlement Preparer → Close Owner\nKeep journal support, business purpose, preparer, approver, posting reference and post-posting check. The same person cannot draft and authorize; Reviewer checks the journal register for completeness.\nC5 Evidence / access / change — each period\nControl owner → Accounting Reviewer; access change requester → Close Owner\nEvidence package: control ID, period, SAP extract/run or batch ID, version, preparer/date, reviewer/date/comments, exceptions/status/impact and resolution or escalation. Role-based least-privilege access restricts SAP, BlackLine and evidence folders to duties. Close Owner reviews access monthly and removes/changes access for departures or role changes. Changes to mappings, logic, templates or thresholds require owner request, independent test/review, version record and Close Owner approval before use.\n\n5. Data flow, evidence and contingency\nEnd-state data boundary: SAP remains authoritative for grain-settlement detail and all member PII. The only data allowed from SAP into BlackLine or its controlled evidence area are period/run identifiers, masked or non-identifying settlement keys, record counts, control totals, ledger/balance references, exception status and links or references to the on-premises evidence location. This minimum proves population completeness, accuracy and workflow status without copying member-level details. Identifying investigation detail stays in on-premises SAP and is referenced, not attached, outside it.\nIf the feed, interface, or system is unavailable, the Settlement Preparer obtains the controlled on-premises SAP extract, records the outage and uses the same C1 comparison in the approved reconciliation register. Do not circulate member PII by email. The Reviewer signs the manual evidence; once the interface returns, the Preparer reruns C1 and reconciles the fallback record to the restored feed. An outage does not convert an open reconciliation into a cleared one.\nThe exception register is the single list of open differences: ID, control, owner, status (Clear/Hold/Escalate), impact assessment, due point, source-correction or accounting-adjustment path, resolution evidence, and escalation. A mandatory control late or incomplete by Day 4 is Escalate; the Close Owner documents the reason and impact and either obtains documented exception disposition for committee visibility or does not declare close complete. Calendar passage alone never closes a period.\n6. Transition state — controlled bridge\nUntil the selected end state is operating, SAP is the authoritative operational and accounting source; the approved, access-restricted reconciliation register is the authoritative control record. Temporary spreadsheets are stored only in the controlled on-premises evidence location, carry period/version/owner, and are not email attachments. Email may notify a reviewer that work is ready, but it is not evidence or a source of truth. C1–C5, role separation, review evidence and the PII boundary apply unchanged to manual work.\nMigrate a control only after the Close Owner documents that its end-state workflow has been configured within the purchased scope, tested against a period-tagged SAP extract, independently reviewed, and produces the required evidence package. Then mark the transition control “retired” in the register and retain its last approved package. Do not run two competing records: any temporary duplicate is cross-referenced to the authoritative record and retired by that test.\n7. End-state design — selected configuration\nUse SAP on premises as the authoritative grain-settlement system and BlackLine as a limited close-control workflow and evidence layer for the four named roles. Feed it only the minimum non-PII control dataset described above; retain PII-bearing detail and source evidence in SAP/on-premises storage. The workflow tracks preparation, independent review, exceptions, deadlines and certification; it does not replace SAP, authorize uncontrolled data extraction, or remove the need for human review. This concentrates scarce licence capacity on key reconciliations and status while keeping manual investigation local, making the five-day sequence achievable without assigning one person both preparation and approval.\n8. Rejected alternatives and approval rationale\nRejected alternative\nWhy it fails Ashgrove’s stated constraints\nQuarryfield Collective’s cheaper, simpler proposal\nLeaving more preparation and review with one person creates self-review risk, weakens review evidence and overloads the five-day close. Spreadsheet/email circulation also makes authoritative status and the PII boundary hard to defend.\nBroad cloud automation / SAP replacement approach\nSAP must remain in place, and member PII cannot leave the on-premises environment. A broad transfer of settlement detail would exceed the minimum control dataset and assumes facts not established.\nFully manual long-term close\nManual evidence can be controlled during transition, but a permanent email-and-spreadsheet process leaves version, exception, review and workload discipline too dependent on individuals for a four-person five-day close.\n\nThe selected design deliberately trades broad automation for a narrow, reviewable control layer: it preserves SAP and privacy, limits licence consumption to high-value workflow, retains independent review, and keeps manual investigation where PII resides. Desirable later enhancements—additional automation, analytics, wider workflow coverage, and richer dashboards—may proceed only if they preserve C1–C5 and do not consume capacity needed for the mandatory close.\n9. Single configuration decision\nApprove the end-state configuration: on-premises SAP remains the authoritative grain-settlement and PII environment; BlackLine is a limited, role-based non-PII close-control workflow and evidence layer, operated by the four roles above with C1–C5 mandatory and the Close Owner as the sole close certifier.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted artifact is a readable DOCX with the exact requested filename.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"File name and successful document opening.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the top-level file is named ashgrove_close_control_design.docx and can be rendered."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document is no more than eight rendered pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX with a standard office engine and count pages."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document is no more than 2,200 words, excluding ordinary headers and footers.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document word count.","grader_hint":"Use a DOCX word-count tool and exclude only headers and footers."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document names Ashgrove Cooperative and the SAP grain-settlement environment.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Organization and system references.","grader_hint":"Search the body text for Ashgrove Cooperative and SAP or grain-settlement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies the five-business-day close requirement.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Close calendar or timing statement.","grader_hint":"Accept an explicit five-business-day target or an equivalent day-by-day close deadline."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document recognizes that the SAP instance remains in place while BlackLine is introduced.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition architecture statement.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit statement that SAP is retained and BlackLine is an introduced layer or platform."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document presents one and only one recommended target configuration.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation section and decision language.","grader_hint":"A preferred configuration must be clearly selected; alternatives must not be framed as co-equal recommendations."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The selected configuration is explicitly presented as the end-state design.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"End-state label or equivalent.","grader_hint":"Accept 'end state', 'target state', or unambiguous equivalent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The transition design and end-state design are visibly distinct and connected by stated migration assumptions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Separate transition and end-state content.","grader_hint":"The reader should be able to tell what applies before implementation and what applies after it, and why the transition can lead to the target."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The target design maps the close process to the five-business-day deadline without requiring an implausible simultaneous workload.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Close calendar, control timing, and workload allocation.","grader_hint":"Look for sequencing or day ownership that makes the deadline operational rather than merely repeating it."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design identifies the four-person accounting team as four separable roles or duties, even if one person holds more than one role at different points.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Role matrix or equivalent.","grader_hint":"The artifact must show who prepares, reviews, approves or owns exceptions, and oversees the close."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"No individual is assigned incompatible preparation and independent review or approval of the same material reconciliation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Role-to-control mapping.","grader_hint":"Trace at least the main SAP-to-close reconciliation and journal or exception review for incompatible dual ownership."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design addresses the residual segregation-of-duties risk created by having only four people.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Compensating-control discussion.","grader_hint":"Accept documented compensating review, management oversight, access restriction, or equivalent risk treatment tied to the staffing limit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The artifact defines who owns the overall close and who can declare it complete.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Accountability statement.","grader_hint":"Ownership must be assigned to a role, not left as 'the team'."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each key reconciliation has a preparer, an independent reviewer, a review point, and retained evidence.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Control descriptions or matrix.","grader_hint":"Trace these four attributes for the principal close reconciliations rather than accepting a generic statement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design includes a reconciliation between the SAP grain-settlement interface or source population and the close records.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Interface reconciliation control.","grader_hint":"Look for completeness and/or accuracy reconciliation tied specifically to the grain-settlement feed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The interface reconciliation addresses completeness, accuracy, and duplicate or omitted records rather than only agreeing a final total.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reconciliation procedure description.","grader_hint":"Accept counts, control totals, record-level exception handling, or equivalent evidence covering those risks."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design specifies how unresolved interface differences are prevented from being silently treated as reconciled.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception status and sign-off rules.","grader_hint":"Look for open exception status, documented approval, or a similar explicit treatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design includes review of manual journals or other manual close adjustments.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Manual-entry control.","grader_hint":"A control must cover authorization and review of manual adjustments, not merely mention journals."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design addresses cutoff and late-arriving settlement information.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cutoff or late-data control.","grader_hint":"Look for a defined review or escalation of transactions received after the relevant close boundary."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document addresses unreconciled balances and aged reconciling items with ownership and escalation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Aged-item treatment.","grader_hint":"It must say who follows up, when escalation occurs or how aging is monitored; a vague 'investigate differences' is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design includes evidence of review, not only evidence that a reconciliation file was prepared.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Review sign-off or audit-trail requirement.","grader_hint":"Accept dated sign-off, review comments, workflow approval, or equivalent evidence of what the reviewer did."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Evidence is retained in a way that links the control, period, preparer, reviewer, exceptions, and resolution.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence-retention design.","grader_hint":"The artifact should specify a traceable evidence package or metadata set, not merely say 'retain documentation'."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design prohibits member PII from leaving the on-premises SAP environment.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data-boundary statement.","grader_hint":"Look for an unambiguous prohibition or equivalent boundary applying to exports, uploads, or integrations."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design distinguishes the data needed for control evidence from member-level PII and minimizes the latter.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data-minimization treatment.","grader_hint":"Accept aggregation, masking, tokenization, or keeping identifying detail on premises when evidence can be retained without it."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Access to close evidence and relevant systems is role-based and restricted to the duties required.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Access-control design.","grader_hint":"Look for least privilege, role-based access, or equivalent allocation tied to the four roles."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The artifact addresses access changes, departures, or periodic access review.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Access lifecycle control.","grader_hint":"A complete design includes at least one mechanism for removing or reviewing inappropriate access."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design does not assume that BlackLine may receive member PII or unrestricted SAP data.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Architecture and data-flow statements.","grader_hint":"Confirm the recommended configuration respects the stated privacy boundary without inventing a product feature."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The target design states what data or evidence crosses the SAP-to-BlackLine boundary and why that minimum is sufficient.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data-flow description.","grader_hint":"The boundary must be concrete enough for implementation review and must explain the control purpose of the transferred content."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design treats BlackLine licence tiers as a capacity or scope constraint without asserting unsupported tier capabilities.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Licence tradeoff discussion.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit assumptions or a tier-neutral decision rationale rather than fabricated product specifications."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The selected configuration explains why its automation, manual work, and licence consumption fit the four-person team.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Capacity rationale.","grader_hint":"The rationale must connect staffing and licence constraints to the selected design, not simply claim that it is efficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The artifact identifies the controls that are mandatory for reliable and compliant close execution.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Must-control classification.","grader_hint":"Mandatory controls should cover the principal risks and be distinguishable from enhancements."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The artifact identifies desirable or phased enhancements separately from mandatory controls.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Should-control classification.","grader_hint":"Accept 'should', 'phase two', or equivalent only when it is clearly non-mandatory."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The must-versus-should boundary is defensible against the privacy, segregation-of-duties, close-timing, and evidence risks in this scenario.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Classification rationale.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the boundary reflects risk and feasibility rather than labeling every desirable practice mandatory."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states what happens when a mandatory control cannot be completed on time.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Control-failure treatment.","grader_hint":"Look for escalation, documented override, delayed close, or equivalent; silence is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Exceptions are logged with a responsible owner, status, impact assessment, and resolution or escalation path.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception-management design.","grader_hint":"A generic exception log without ownership and disposition does not satisfy the item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design includes management or finance-committee visibility over material unresolved exceptions and close status.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Oversight reporting.","grader_hint":"Accept a close certification, dashboard, exception summary, or equivalent management view."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design defines a close-completion or certification condition that depends on control status and not merely calendar passage.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Completion criteria.","grader_hint":"Look for required reconciliations, approvals, and exception disposition before close is certified."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The transition arrangement preserves segregation of duties and evidence even while some controls remain manual.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition control design.","grader_hint":"Manual workarounds must not be a blanket exception to review, access, or evidence requirements."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The transition design prevents parallel SAP, spreadsheet, email, and BlackLine records from becoming uncontrolled competing sources of truth.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition source-of-truth rule.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit ownership of the authoritative record and reconciliation of any temporary duplicate artifacts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states how the team will know that a transition control has been retired or replaced by the end-state control.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Control migration or exit criteria.","grader_hint":"Accept defined evidence of activation, testing, or approval before retirement of the old practice."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design addresses interface failure, delayed feed, or unavailable system conditions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Business-continuity exception treatment.","grader_hint":"The response must identify a controlled fallback and subsequent reconciliation, not merely say to contact IT."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The fallback for a system or interface failure does not authorize uncontrolled email circulation of member PII.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Fallback privacy treatment.","grader_hint":"Trace the privacy boundary into the contingency arrangement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The artifact includes control over changes to reconciliation logic, mappings, thresholds, or close templates.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Change-management control.","grader_hint":"Look for authorization, testing, versioning, or review of changes affecting control outcomes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design provides a way to identify the source period and version of the data used in a reconciliation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Traceability metadata.","grader_hint":"Accept period, run, batch, extract date, or equivalent identifiers."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design includes review of unusual or high-risk balances or movements in addition to mechanical agreement.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Analytical review control.","grader_hint":"Look for variance, reasonableness, trend, or unusual-item review with a defined response."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The target design explains how close controls interact with the grain-settlement module's operational data rather than treating SAP as a generic ledger.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Domain-specific interface treatment.","grader_hint":"Look for settlement-specific population, timing, member, contract, delivery, or equivalent operational considerations without invented data."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design distinguishes source-system correction from accounting adjustment and assigns each to an appropriate owner.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Correction and adjustment treatment.","grader_hint":"The artifact should prevent accounting entries from masking a source-interface defect."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The artifact gives frontline staff unambiguous decision criteria for when to clear, hold, escalate, or document a reconciliation difference.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Operational decision guidance.","grader_hint":"Judge whether a new team member could execute the response without asking what each status means."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design identifies the minimum evidence a reviewer must inspect before signing off a reconciliation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reviewer guidance.","grader_hint":"Look for specific evidence categories such as source population, comparison, exceptions, and preparer work."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rejected alternatives include the simpler or cheaper Quarryfield Collective proposal or an unmistakably equivalent low-control alternative.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternatives section.","grader_hint":"The alternative must be recognizable as the proposal described in the request, not a newly invented straw man."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"At least two rejected alternatives are described with specific failure points against the stated constraints.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rejected-alternatives analysis.","grader_hint":"Each alternative needs a concrete tradeoff involving staffing, privacy, timing, interface, evidence, or licence scope."},{"rubric_item_id":"R53","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rejection analysis does not rely on unsupported cost figures, product claims, legal citations, or invented operational facts.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Supported rationale.","grader_hint":"Judge whether claims stay within the facts supplied and ordinary control-design reasoning."},{"rubric_item_id":"R54","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document explains the principal tradeoff that made the selected configuration preferable to the rejected alternatives.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision rationale.","grader_hint":"The recommendation should show a reasoned balance rather than a preference statement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R55","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The artifact is instructional and independently usable by a frontline accounting employee while remaining concise enough for board review.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered document as a whole.","grader_hint":"Judge clarity, defined terms, role specificity, decision usability, and whether the document avoids unexplained control jargon."},{"rubric_item_id":"R56","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is internally consistent: the selected roles, data boundary, transition model, mandatory controls, timing, and rejected-alternative reasoning do not contradict one another.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-document consistency review.","grader_hint":"Trace the recommendation across the control matrix, transition section, privacy treatment, staffing allocation, and conclusion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R57","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document minimizes sensitive information and uses no real member identifiers or unnecessary personal data.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered and extracted document content.","grader_hint":"Confirm that no real-looking member records, account numbers, addresses, or other unnecessary identifiers are included."},{"rubric_item_id":"R58","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The final section ends with the single selected configuration or decision, not a list of next steps.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ending of the document.","grader_hint":"Inspect the final substantive paragraph; it should reaffirm one decision rather than defer the decision or end with action items."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is a fictional cooperative accounting-control design assignment involving a legacy SAP grain-settlement environment, a planned BlackLine implementation, member privacy, and a small finance team. The artifact must serve both operational staff and a board finance committee.","difficulty_sources":["segregation-of-duties tradeoffs within a four-person accounting team","designing a workable transition state and an end-state control model","drawing a defensible must-versus-should boundary under a five-business-day close","protecting member PII while retaining audit evidence","rejecting alternatives without relying on unsupported cost or system claims"],"authoring_plan":{"purpose":"Create a compact instructional control-design briefing that demonstrates professional judgment without requiring supplied data or live research.","reference_files":[],"gold_design":{"overall_approach":"Construct a fictional but operationally credible target design around a role-based close-control model in which SAP remains the system of record for the grain-settlement population, BlackLine is used only to the extent supported by the chosen licence assumption, and member-identifying data remains on premises. The exact vendor tier and numeric thresholds should be stated as assumptions or implementation decisions, not as facts.","transition_state":["Retain controlled SAP extracts and a versioned reconciliation register while the platform change is underway.","Permit manual evidence only with named preparer and independent reviewer, controlled access, and a clear authoritative record.","Define how temporary spreadsheets or email notifications are controlled, minimized, and retired.","Include a fallback for delayed or failed interfaces that preserves privacy and requires later reconciliation."],"end_state":["Show a stable flow from SAP grain-settlement data to the close-control environment.","Assign preparation, review, exception ownership, and management certification across four roles without incompatible self-review.","Keep only the minimum non-PII or masked data needed outside the on-premises boundary.","Use system workflow or controlled evidence where available, while avoiding claims about unverified BlackLine features."],"must_should_boundary":{"mandatory":["Reliable completeness and accuracy reconciliation of the grain-settlement interface.","Independent review of key reconciliations and manual adjustments.","Role-based access and segregation-of-duties treatment, including a compensating control where staffing makes perfect separation impossible.","Protection of member PII and a clear SAP-to-platform data boundary.","Exception ownership, escalation, evidence retention, and close certification.","Cutoff and late-data treatment sufficient to support the five-business-day close."],"desirable":["Additional automation, broader workflow coverage, enhanced analytics, or expanded dashboarding that improves efficiency but is not necessary to prevent a material control failure.","Convenience features that consume licence capacity without changing the core privacy, review, or completeness controls."],"rationale":"The gold should explain that the boundary is based on risk to completeness, accuracy, privacy, authorization, and close certification, balanced against four-person capacity and the five-day deadline. It should not classify every efficiency improvement as mandatory."},"rejected_alternatives":["A cheaper or simpler Quarryfield Collective-style design that leaves too much preparation and review with one person or relies on uncontrolled spreadsheet/email circulation.","A broad cloud automation design that transfers member-level PII or assumes the SAP instance can be replaced.","A fully manual design that preserves segregation and privacy but cannot reliably meet the five-business-day close or sustain evidence quality.","Optionally, a maximal BlackLine licence design that improves automation but is not justified by the stated budget or staffing constraint."],"professional_content_to_expect":["A close calendar tied to five business days.","A role matrix for four accounting roles.","Control objectives, control owners, reviewers, timing, evidence, and exception responses.","SAP-to-close completeness and accuracy reconciliation.","Manual journal, cutoff, unusual-balance, aged-item, and source-correction controls.","Access lifecycle and change-management controls.","PII minimization, masking or aggregation, and controlled fallback handling.","Transition exit criteria and end-state ownership.","A single recommendation and specific reasons for rejecting alternatives."],"unsupported_claims_to_avoid":["Exact BlackLine licence features or tier entitlements.","Unprovided monetary thresholds or legal requirements presented as externally mandated.","Real member names, account numbers, addresses, or other personal records.","A claim that a control is effective merely because a spreadsheet exists.","A claim that a reviewer is independent when the same person prepared and approved the item."]},"gold_derivation":"Review the reference solution by rendering it, counting pages and words, then walking the role matrix, transition/end-state distinction, data flow, mandatory/should classification, exception logic, alternative analysis, and final decision. Every relational rubric item must be traceable to a distinct passage or control relationship. The gold passes if it satisfies all credit items without depending on a hidden literal answer, exact vendor tier, or invented fact."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"control_design_docx","filename":"ashgrove_close_control_design.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A concise instructional briefing and target month-end close-and-reconciliation control design for Ashgrove Cooperative, including transition and end-state arrangements, the mandatory-versus-desirable boundary, and rejected alternatives.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be written for both the four-person accounting team and the board finance committee.","Must contain exactly one recommended target configuration.","Must distinguish the transition state from the end state.","Must explain rejected alternatives and their shortcomings.","Must not invent unsupported licence capabilities, legal requirements, financial figures, or member data.","Must be no more than eight pages and 2,200 words.","Must be saved at the top level of the working directory using exactly the specified filename."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"evaluate_compliance","work_product":"compliance_assessment","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world_with_assumptions"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"accountants_auditors","sector":"professional_scientific_technical_services","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":6.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Have an accounting-controls reviewer and an instructional-document reviewer independently inspect the rendered DOCX against every rubric item, then resolve disagreements by tracing the claim to the supplied scenario and ordinary professional control practice. The reference solution should select one coherent configuration, not a predetermined product-specific answer.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["Because no source files or vendor licence documentation are supplied, the rubric evaluates defensible control architecture and careful assumptions rather than exact BlackLine product functionality.","Different competent practitioners may choose different workable configurations; the rubric therefore emphasizes explicit tradeoffs, segregation of duties, privacy, timing, evidence, and internal consistency."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:51:40+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:51:40+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:51:40+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:51:40+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:51:40+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/c84b497217bd0bc1","declared_deliverables":["ashgrove_close_control_design.docx"]};
